For an absolutely convergent series of real numbers $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n$, define its achievement set (set of subsums) by
$$E(x_n) := \Big\{\, y \in \mathbb{R} : \exists\, A \subseteq \mathbb{N} \text{ such that } y = \sum_{n \in A} x_n \,\Big\}.$$
It is a classical fact that, when $(x_n)$ is a sequence of nonzero real numbers tending to $0$ such that the series converges absolutely, $E(x_n)$ is a non-empty compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$ that must be exactly one of the following four topological types:
(i) a finite set;
(ii) a finite union of nondegenerate closed intervals;
(iii) homeomorphic to the Cantor ternary set (a "Cantor set");
(iv) a Cantorval, i.e., a non-empty compact perfect set $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ such that both endpoints of every connected component of $C$ are accumulation points of one-point components of $C$, and such that $C$ has nontrivial interior but is not a finite union of intervals.

Problem. Provide a complete characterization, expressed directly in terms of the generating sequence $(x_n)$, that determines which of the four topological types (i)–(iv) the achievement set $E(x_n)$ belongs to. In particular, give necessary and sufficient conditions on $(x_n)$ for $E(x_n)$ to be (a) a finite set, (b) a finite union of closed intervals, (c) a Cantor set, and (d) a Cantorval.

